{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ae52635d81bfe81d","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8005\n3\tepic_count: 5\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# RHAISTRAT-8005 Decomposition — EvalHub UI: Real-Time Evaluation Job Progress and State Visibility\n11\t\n12\t## Triage Note\n13\t\n14\tThe strategy is S-sized, affects a single active component (the eval-hub-ui plugin inside the odh-dashboard monorepo), and is implemented primarily by one team, so below-threshold was considered. It does not apply, for two independent reasons (Step 0 escape conditions):\n15\t\n16\t1. **Genuine unknowns.** The strategy explicitly flags two open questions as \"needs validation\" in both its Assumptions and Open Questions tables: (a) whether `status.benchmarks[]` is populated in the detail endpoint response while a job is still running, and (b) what values `status.message.message_code` takes for pre-start vs runtime failures. The answer to (a) determines whether the detail-polling layer and benchmark-level progress display exist at all or are replaced by an elapsed-time-only display; the answer to (b) determines whether the pre-start/runtime failure distinction is implementable. Both change what downstream epics do.\n17\t2. **Multiple distinct work streams.** The scope spans frontend code implementation (ODH Dashboard team) and substantive UX design authoring — the \"progress indicator UX specification\" listed as to-be-created in Supporting Documentation, plus state icon/color specifications, owned by UX/Design. These are architecturally distinct work products warranting separate review cycles.\n18\t\n19\tDocumentation-only triage does not apply: eval-hub-ui requires code changes.\n20\t\n21\t## Staff Engineer Input Overrides (Step 1.5)\n22\t\n23\tThe Staff Engineer Input was parsed first and takes precedence. It confirms the two-layer polling architecture and resolves process-level questions that would otherwise have been unknowns; these are recorded as fixed parameters rather than investigations:\n24\t\n25\t- Latency window: 10 seconds is sufficient for state-transition visibility.\n26\t- Page Visibility API: polling pauses when the tab is backgrounded — approved to save resources.\n27\t- Concurrency: poll only jobs on the currently displayed table page (client-side paginated, default 20 rows, options 5/10/20); maximum 5 concurrent detail fetches in round-robin, switching to the next job as one completes; timer for the next cycle starts when all calls complete. Scalability beyond this is explicitly deferred for this release.\n28\t- Data constraint: no data beyond what the two existing API endpoints provide — no EvalHub Server API extension epics are generated (out-of-scope items confirming this were honored).\n29\t\n30\tDivergence log: the Staff Engineer Input does not diverge from the AI-generated Technical Approach in substance — it constrains and confirms it. Notably, the Staff Engineer's field-availability table documents which fields are present in the detail endpoint response schema, but does **not** resolve whether `status.benchmarks[]` entries are populated mid-run versus only at completion, nor the semantics of `status.message.message_code` values. Those two questions remain genuine unknowns flagged by the strategy author and are decomposed as Investigation epics.\n31\t\n32\t## Component Mapping Notes\n33\t\n34\t- Canonical component `AI Evaluations` is used for all eval-hub-ui plugin work and both investigations. The canonical list (`.context/rhai-components.txt`) has no `eval-hub-ui` or `odh-dashboard` entry; `AI Evaluations` is the closest canonical name for the EvalHub evaluations UI and the EvalHub Server API behavior under investigation. The span (odh-dashboard monorepo plugin + EvalHub Server API) is noted per epic.\n35\t- The UX specification epic uses canonical component `UXD` (owned by UX/Design).\n36\t- **Passive component handling**: odh-dashboard (BFF proxy) is passive (\"verify pass-through, no code change expected\") and is owned by the same team (ODH Dashboard) as the active eval-hub-ui work, so its validation is an acceptance criterion on the nearest active epic (the branch-A detail-polling epic, which is where detail response fields are consumed) rather than a separate validation epic.\n37\t- Both eval-hub and odh-dashboard have architecture context files (`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/`), so both are in-platform — no repo/konflux onboarding chain is generated.\n38\t\n39\t## Epic List\n40\t\n41\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n42\t| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n43\t| RHAISTRAT-8005-E001 | Validate mid-run availability of benchmark status in the EvalHub job detail API | Investigation | TrustyAI / EvalHub | P0 |\n44\t| RHAISTRAT-8005-E002 | Catalogue EvalHub failure message codes for pre-start vs runtime failures | Investigation | TrustyAI / EvalHub | P2 |\n45\t| RHAISTRAT-8005-E003 | Progress indicator and job state UX specification for the EvalHub Evaluations page | Implementation | UX/Design | P0 |\n46\t| RHAISTRAT-8005-E004 | State visual differentiation for the EvalHub evaluations job list | Implementation | ODH Dashboard | P0 |\n47\t| RHAISTRAT-8005-E005 | Pre-start vs runtime failure distinction in the evaluations job list | Implementation | ODH Dashboard | P2 |\n48\t| RHAISTRAT-8005-BRANCH-A-E006 | Detail-polling layer with benchmark progress and elapsed time for in-progress evaluation jobs (branch A) | Implementation | ODH Dashboard | P0 |\n49\t| RHAISTRAT-8005-BRANCH-B-E006 | Elapsed-time progress display for running evaluation jobs (branch B) | Implementation | ODH Dashboard | P1 |\n50\t\n51\tMain-plan epics: E001–E005 (`epic_count=5`). Branch epics BRANCH-A-E006 / BRANCH-B-E006 are conditional alternatives selected by the E001 investigation outcome; they are attached to the investigation as a plan document, not standalone Jira issues, and are excluded from the main-plan critical path.\n52\t\n53\t## Dependency DAG\n54\t\n55\t```mermaid\n56\tgraph TD\n57\t    E001[\"E001 Investigation: benchmarks[] mid-run availability\"] --> BRA[\"BRANCH-A-E006 Detail polling + benchmark progress\"]\n58\t    E001 --> BRB[\"BRANCH-B-E006 Elapsed-time-only display\"]\n59\t    E002[\"E002 Investigation: message_code failure semantics\"] --> E005[\"E005 Pre-start vs runtime failure distinction\"]\n60\t    E003[\"E003 UX specification\"] --> E004[\"E004 State visual differentiation\"]\n61\t    E003 --> BRA\n62\t    E003 --> BRB\n63\t    E004 --> E005\n64\t```\n65\t\n66\tRoots (no dependencies): E001, E002, E003 — all three start in parallel.\n67\t\n68\t## DAG Justification\n69\t\n70\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n71\t| --- | --- | --- |\n72\t| E001 → BRANCH-A-E006, BRANCH-B-E006 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | Whether `status.benchmarks[]` is populated mid-run determines which of the two progress-display implementations exists. Binary bounded outcome → conditional branch epics. Both branch epics set `gated_by=RHAISTRAT-8005-E001` with `gate_failure_impact` recorded. |\n73\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The `message_code` catalogue determines whether the pre-start/runtime failure distinction is implementable; if values are indistinguishable, E005 is removed and the defined fallback (single \"Failed\" state with `status.message` text) applies — recorded as `gate_failure_impact.action=remove`. |\n74\t| E003 → E004 | Rule 12/13 (artifact consumer serial) | The state differentiation UI builds against the UX specification's state icon/color/label treatments; the spec is a build-against artifact. |\n75\t| E003 → BRANCH-A-E006, BRANCH-B-E006 | Rule 13 (artifact consumer serial) | Both progress-display variants render per the UX progress indicator pattern (elapsed time primary, benchmark count supplementary). |\n76\t| E004 → E005 | Rule 12/13 (framework → consumer) | The failure distinction refines the state rendering surface built by the state differentiation work; the surface must not be built twice, and the refinement extends it. |\n77\t| E001 ∥ E002 ∥ E003 (no edges) | Rule 14 (parallel) | Independent inputs: an API behavior validation, a failure-code catalogue, and a design specification. The UX spec does not wait on E001 because the strategy's risk mitigation pre-decides elapsed-time-primary presentation, which holds under both outcomes. |\n78\t| E004 not gated by E001 or E002 | Rule 14 | State differentiation uses only list-endpoint fields (`status.state`, `status.message`) whose availability is confirmed; it proceeds regardless of either investigation outcome. |\n79\t| No onboarding chain | Rule 20 N/A | eval-hub and odh-dashboard both have architecture context files — components are in-platform; the plugin is already onboarded (per strategy prerequisites). |\n80\t\n81\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n82\t\n83\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n84\t| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n85\t| HLR-1 Auto-refresh mechanism with state transitions (list polling + detail polling) | P0 | E004, RHAISTRAT-8005-BRANCH-A-E006 | E004 delivers top-level state transitions via the existing 30s list polling; the detail-polling clause is delivered by branch A. In branch B the detail-polling clause is not implementable against the existing API (see Health Warnings). |\n86\t| HLR-2 Visual distinction of queued/running/failed/completed states | P0 | E004 | From list-endpoint `status.state`. |\n87\t| HLR-3 Non-blocking error handling during detail polling | P0 | RHAISTRAT-8005-BRANCH-A-E006 | Retry with exponential backoff per the autorag pattern, stale data preserved, inline warning. |\n88\t| HLR-4 Benchmark-level progress display (X/N) | P1 | RHAISTRAT-8005-BRANCH-A-E006 | Branch-A only; not deliverable in branch B (completion-only population). |\n89\t| HLR-5 Elapsed time display for running jobs | P1 | RHAISTRAT-8005-BRANCH-A-E006, RHAISTRAT-8005-BRANCH-B-E006 | From earliest per-benchmark `started_at` in branch A; from `resource.created_at` in branch B. |\n90\t| HLR-6 Polling pauses on backgrounded tab (Page Visibility API) | P1 | RHAISTRAT-8005-BRANCH-A-E006 | Applies to the new detail-polling layer. |\n91\t| HLR-7 Pre-start vs runtime failure distinction via `message_code` | P2 | E005 | E004 provides the fallback rendering (single \"Failed\" with `status.message` text) if the distinction proves unimplementable. |\n92\t\n93\tAll seven HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. E003 (UX specification) supports HLR-2, HLR-4, HLR-5, and HLR-7 but delivers none alone — it is a prerequisite artifact, reflected as a dependency edge rather than primary coverage.\n94\t\n95\t## Conditional Branches\n96\t\n97\tThe E001 investigation has two bounded outcomes, each selecting a branch for the progress-display epic slot E006:\n98\t\n99\t- **Branch A — `status.benchmarks[]` populated mid-run**: build the full detail-polling layer (10s cycle, max-5 concurrent round-robin over visible-page in-progress jobs, React Query retry-with-backoff per the autorag pattern, Page Visibility pause) plus benchmark-level progress and elapsed-time display. Priority P0 (carries the P0 detail-polling and error-handling HLRs).\n100\t- **Branch B — populated only on completion**: no mid-run detail polling; display elapsed time computed from `resource.created_at` (list endpoint) as the primary progress indicator. Priority P1.\n101\t\n102\tBoth branch epics depend on the gating investigation and on the UX specification, and each records `gate_failure_impact` describing the alternative outcome.\n103\t\n104\tE002's outcome gates E005 in the main plan (rather than branches) because the unfavorable outcome removes the epic entirely: the fallback — a single \"Failed\" state with `status.message` text — is the baseline already delivered by the state differentiation work, so no branch epic is warranted for it.\n105\t\n106\t## Health Warnings\n107\t\n108\t- **HLR coverage is branch-conditional**: if E001 finds `status.benchmarks[]` is only populated on completion, HLR-4 (benchmark progress, P1), HLR-6 (detail-polling visibility pause, P1), and the detail-polling clause of HLR-1 (P0) are not deliverable against the existing API. Per the Staff Engineer constraint of no API extensions, this requires a strategy-level descope decision — flagged for PM/Engineering validation (the strategy's acceptance criteria are themselves marked \"requires PM/Engineering validation\").\n109\t- **Pending human process gates**: Architecture Review, Licence Validation, UXD Support, and Performance Team Support are all \"Pending human review\" in the strategy's prerequisites. These are strategy-level sign-offs, not decomposable technical unknowns; they are noted here rather than modeled as epics. UXD support is partially materialized as the UX specification epic.\n110\t- **Scope trap (mitigated)**: single-benchmark jobs would show only 0%/100% benchmark progress. The strategy's mitigation — elapsed time as the primary progress indicator — is folded into the UX specification epic's scope so both branches present elapsed time primarily.\n111\t- **Validation risk folded into AC, not an investigation**: the React Query retry pattern from autorag has not been tested in the eval-hub Module Federation context. This does not change which epics exist (implementation proceeds the same way; failure = fix-and-retry), so it is an acceptance criterion on the branch-A epic (simulate 5xx errors from the BFF on individual detail endpoints), not an Investigation epic.\n112\t- **Priority inversion note (informational)**: E003 (UX specification) is P0 only by inheritance — it blocks the P0 state differentiation work. Its intrinsic effort is small; teams may descope it to a lightweight PatternFly-alignment review if that preserves the P0 schedule.\n113\t- **No tiered delivery**: no external dependency has uncertain timing (PatternFly and React Query both exist and are in use), so no Tier 1/Tier 2 split applies.\n114\t- All required strategy sections were present; no missing-section warnings.\n115\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b9ca683fd5a24274","agent_id":"a19e66e98275a5ef3","session_id":"f9ae5287-b92a-4025-94c1-a1778be86910","uuid":"b0eedf45-d9d3-44f9-99e0-89f854a40fb2","timestamp":"2026-10-22T07:31:34.043Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-ae52635d81bfe81d","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8005 decomposition"}
