{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b9f9bea0cffcc2e2","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8005\n3\tscore: 14\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues: []\n7\terror: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-8005\n10\t\n11\t## Review Summary\n12\t\n13\tScore: 14/14 — pass\n14\tRecommendation: accept\n15\t\n16\tTriage consistency: `triage` is null (full decomposition) with 5 main-plan epics and 2 conditional branch epics — no triage contradiction. Contract validator: OK (epic body sections, canonical component names, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, and priority inheritance all pass; no `VIOLATION:` lines).\n17\t\n18\tThe decomposition is a faithful and structurally correct translation of the strategy. Both genuine unknowns flagged by the strategy (mid-run `status.benchmarks[]` population; `status.message.message_code` failure semantics) are Investigation epics with correctly wired conditional downstream structure; the Staff Engineer Input overrides (10s cycle, max-5 round-robin over visible-page jobs, Page Visibility pause, no API extensions) are recorded as fixed parameters rather than re-investigated; and the branch-conditional non-deliverability of the detail-polling HLRs in the unfavorable outcome is explicitly escalated as a health warning rather than silently dropped.\n19\t\n20\t## Criterion Details\n21\t\n22\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n23\tAll seven HLRs map to at least one epic in the traceability matrix, and each epic's HLR Traceability section is consistent with the matrix. P0 HLR-1/2/3 → E004 + BRANCH-A-E006; P1 HLR-4/5/6 → BRANCH-A-E006 (+ BRANCH-B-E006 for HLR-5); P2 HLR-7 → E005 with E004 delivering the strategy-defined fallback baseline. Priority inheritance is correct: E003 is P0 because it blocks P0 state-differentiation and branch-A work (validator-confirmed); E002 is P2 and gates only P2 work; E004 (P0) blocking P2 E005 is fine. No priority collapse: the P1 HLRs bundled into branch A (benchmark progress, elapsed time, visibility pause) share the same polling hook and row surface — tightly coupled, correctly bundled per the boundary rules; E003's spec covering HLR-2/4/5/7 is one artifact, not deferrable features. The branch-conditional gap (HLR-4/HLR-6 and the detail-polling clause of HLR-1 undeliverable in branch B) is not a coverage failure: the strategy's Staff Engineer constraint forbids API extensions, and the decomposition flags the required strategy-level descope decision in Health Warnings with PM/Engineering escalation — the best available translation.\n24\t\n25\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n26\tFrontmatter `dependencies` match the summary DAG diagram edge-for-edge in both directions (E001/E002/E003 roots; E003→E004; E002→E005; E004→E005; E001→ both branches; E003→ both branches), and arrows run dependency→dependent per the diagram convention. No cycles, no invalid edges, no main-plan epic depending on a branch node. Both branch files set `branch` (A/B, matching filenames) and `gated_by=RHAISTRAT-8005-E001`, with E001 present in their direct `dependencies`. Every edge is rule-justified: Rule 3 investigation gates for E001/E002 (bounded binary outcome → conditional branches for E001; remove-action gate for E002), Rule 13 build-against artifact for E003's UX specification, and shared-surface refinement for E004→E005. Parallelism is preserved: E001 ∥ E002 ∥ E003 start concurrently; E004 is correctly not gated by either investigation (uses only confirmed list-endpoint fields, explicitly justified); no konflux/repo onboarding chain exists (both components in-platform), so rules 7/8/10 are N/A. Critical path is 3 (E003→E004→E005), one above the S-size heuristic of 1-2, but each edge is rule-mandated (required UX spec artifact → state UI → gated P2 refinement) and the P0-only path is 2 — reasonable for this strategy's structure; noted as an observation, not a defect.\n27\t\n28\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n29\tComponent/team tuples are cleanly separated: E001/E002 investigations (TrustyAI / EvalHub, the strategy's named owners of the open questions), E003 UX spec (UXD / UX/Design), E004/E005/branch epics (AI Evaluations / ODH Dashboard). `AI Evaluations` and `UXD` are verbatim canonical components; the non-obvious mapping (no canonical `eval-hub-ui`/`odh-dashboard`) is logged in the summary as required. The passive BFF-proxy component is correctly handled as an acceptance criterion on the nearest active epic (branch A, where detail fields are consumed) rather than a separate epic, matching the passive-component rule (same owning team). No scope duplication: E004 delivers the complete fallback state surface, E005 is a gated refinement that may not exist — the split is driven by the E002 investigation gate and the strategy's own P2 \"where available\" deferral, not by redundant requirement wording on an unconditional surface, so the presentation-mode duplication pattern does not apply. Work-product types are correctly separated: the UX specification (content authoring) is its own epic, distinct from all code epics. No epic approaches multi-sprint size.\n30\t\n31\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n32\tBoth Investigations genuinely change downstream structure: E001's outcome selects branch A vs branch B (different epics exist), E002's outcome determines whether E005 exists at all. Both have downstream dependents. E003 is correctly an Implementation (produces a concrete artifact — the build-against UX specification) and correctly not `docs-authoring` (a design spec, not Red Hat docs content; Documentation Support is not \"Yes\", so Rule 11 is N/A per the validator). All gating metadata is complete and correct: E005, BRANCH-A-E006, and BRANCH-B-E006 each set `gated_by` to an Investigation that appears in their direct `dependencies`, with both `gate_failure_impact.action` (remove / rewrite / rewrite) and `fallback_approach` populated and semantically accurate. No epic lists an Investigation dependency without `gated_by`; no non-gate epic carries spurious gating fields. The React Query validation risk was correctly downgraded to an AC on branch A (outcome does not change epic structure) rather than an Investigation.\n33\t\n34\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n35\tType-appropriate signal sets are used throughout: E001/E002 carry `investigation_signals` (5-signal rubric); E003–E005 and both branch epics carry `ai_signals` (9-signal rubric). Signal rationale files exist for all seven epics, including both branch epics, and the rationales match the frontmatter values. Values are consistent with the rubric and strategy content: E001/E002 score question_specificity/source_accessibility +1 (binary/enumerable questions answerable from the open EvalHub Server source), local_runnability 0 and cluster_hardware_dependence -1 (empirical confirmation needs a live cluster running a job, but the gating answer is source-readable — correctly scored as peripheral, not gating); E003's repo_access 0 (design document, no target repo — N/A), human_process_gates -1 (UXD authorship/review); E005's open_questions -1 (existence hinges on the unresolved message_code semantics); branch A's open_questions +1 with human_process_gates -1 (pending Performance Team / Architecture Review gates) are all well-justified against the strategy's prerequisites table. Per instructions, `ai_implementability` totals were not checked (pipeline-computed).\n36\t\n37\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n38\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy's acceptance criteria — all seven strategy ACs reappear, allocated to the correct epic, with measurable conditions (counts matching `status.benchmarks[]` entries, 30s interval, 4m12s elapsed computation, dev-tools Network verification for the backgrounded-tab pause, 5-in-flight concurrency cap). Rule-mandated ACs: no epic replaces an existing capability (additive work — Rule 23 N/A), no konflux chain (Rule 25 N/A), no docs-authoring epic (Rule 24 N/A); E003 appropriately carries a spec-acceptance AC (\"completed and accepted by the ODH Dashboard implementing team\"). No external-schedule ACs are present — every AC is an engineering-controlled pass/fail condition. No draft-ID references appear in any epic body (E00x references exist only in frontmatter `dependencies`, where they belong); dependencies are described in plain terms where mentioned. The risk-mitigation validation (simulated 5xx from the BFF) is correctly an AC on branch A with fix-and-retry semantics.\n39\t\n40\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n41\tThe combined epic set accounts for the full strategy scope: two-layer polling (E004 preserves layer 1; branch A builds layer 2), concurrency control, progress model with both timestamp sources, retry-on-error per the autorag pattern, Page Visibility pause, state differentiation with pre-start/runtime distinction, and the auth-flow statement (a no-change constraint, correctly not epicsized). All strategy NFRs are carried: performance parameters into branch A scope/ACs, backwards compatibility as E004/branch-B \"existing polling unchanged\" ACs with graceful degradation. Strategy-level context is not dropped: all four risks are carried forward (elapsed-time-primary in E003, message_code fallback in E004/E005, scalability deferral and React Query validation in branch A); both open questions become Investigations; pending human process gates are surfaced in Health Warnings and in the epics' `human_process_gates` signal values. The conditional branches cover both bounded outcomes of E001, including the partial-population edge case (an explicit E001 AC requires documenting which presentation it supports). Documentation Support is \"Pending human review\", not \"Yes\", so no docs-authoring epic is required. The passive odh-dashboard BFF verification is placed as an AC on branch A, where detail fields are consumed (and is correctly absent from branch B, where no detail fetches occur).\n42\t"}]},"par